meta {
  name: send
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/internal/rcs/send
  body: json
  auth: none
}

body:json {
  {
    "to": "+15551234567",
    "message": "Hi, this is a test message."
  }
}

docs {
  Sends one message over RCS and waits for the phone to confirm delivery.

  **It never falls back to SMS.** If the recipient cannot take RCS the send is
  refused with 409 and nothing goes out — no silent carrier charges, no green
  bubble.

  **It only sends when encryption is confirmed beforehand.** There is no
  setting that relaxes this. The chat must either carry an encrypted protocol
  tombstone, or still be the phone's unfinalized first draft with a capability
  record of `yes`. Anything unclear (`not_yet`, an unreadable capability, no
  tombstone, an iPhone on plain RCS) is refused, never sent. After the send the
  chat is re-read as a check on that confirmation and the result is recorded
  per number (see `send results`); if the check disagrees, the number is never
  auto-sent to again.

  ```json
  {
    "success": true,
    "message": "sent",
    "data": {
      "to": "+15551234567",
      "sent": true,
      "transport": "RCS",
      "outcome": "SUCCESS_E2EE",
      "finalStatus": "OUTGOING_DELIVERED",
      "messageId": "1050",
      "check": { "e2ee": "unknown", "e2eeCapability": "yes", "...": "" },
      "after": { "e2ee": "on", "...": "" }
    }
  }
  ```

  `transport` is derived from the phone's own message type: **4 is RCS, 1 is
  SMS**. It should always read RCS here; anything else means the guarantee
  above did not hold and is recorded as `SUCCESS_SMS`. `outcome` is the
  verdict — `SUCCESS_E2EE`, `SUCCESS_RCS`, `SUCCESS_SMS` or
  `SUCCESS_UNVERIFIED`, see `send results` — `check` is what eligibility saw
  before the send and `after` is the chat re-read once the message was out.

  ## Blocking and timeouts

  This waits up to `rcs.wait_seconds` (default 20, capped at 20 by the server's
  HTTP write timeout) for the phone to report delivery. Budget for that.

  ## Throughput

  One phone manages a few messages per second. There is no queue and no rate
  limiting here — the caller owns that. Bursting a consumer Google account can
  trip anti-abuse and get it limited.

  ## Character set

  Stick to ASCII. Non-ASCII characters are mangled somewhere below our code in
  the Google Messages layer — an em dash comes out as a replacement character.
  Accents and emoji are not safe.

  Errors:
  - 400 — `to` is not E.164, or `message` is empty
  - 403 — the number is not on `rcs.allowlist_phones` (when that list is set)
  - 409 — refused, **nothing sent**. The error names the reason:
    `not_rcs`, `not_e2ee`, `rcs_disabled_on_sim`, `conversation_read_only`,
    `known_unencrypted` (all permanent; the last is decided here from `send
    results`, before the phone is asked), or `phone_not_responding`,
    `session_not_active`, `rcs_pending`, `e2ee_pending` (transient, worth
    retrying a minute later — `rcs_pending` is a fresh chat the phone has not
    looked up yet, which reads as SMS until it has; see check eligibility for
    what `e2ee_pending` means; a pending chat is always refused, never sent to.
    An iPhone on plain RCS never leaves `e2ee_pending`). Callers should retry
    transient refusals themselves.
  - 503 — rcs disabled, no phone paired, or the send results could not be
    read (refused rather than risk a number on record)
  - 502 — the phone could not be reached
}
